# Direction 4, checked — and it need not repack at all
# Measured 2026-09-08, n=3 x 8 rounds
#
# forge/syncer/tests/push_chain.rs::measure_whether_the_residue_direction_5_keeps_grows
#
# The model's direction 4 plans a FRESH pack: FoldPlan requires
# `holds[f] = {}` (ForgeSync.tla:925), so the reclaim TLC proved safe
# always pays `pack-objects --all --indexed-objects --write-bitmap-index`
# over the whole repository (gitcmd.rs:463) plus the upload of its
# output, blocking Importing -> Serving.
#
# But the RULE it proves never asks the coverer to be new:
#   D == {q \in S : (holds[q] \cap fold[s].at) \subseteq holds[f]}
# If a pack the snapshot ALREADY names covers the reachable set, every
# other named pack can be unnamed for the cost of a snapshot CAS.

round  packs    named B  redundant   covering pack already named?
    0      2      19627       9768   YES 80a339d1 (9859 B) -> 9768 B unnamable for a CAS
    1      4      39165      28919   YES 6a03e4d2 (10246 B) -> 28919 B
    2      6      58704      48072   YES 42d68c1c (10632 B) -> 48072 B
    3      8      78244      67226   YES 96469d49 (11018 B) -> 67226 B
    4     10      97784      86380   YES 97a88fc8 (11404 B) -> 86380 B
    5     12     117339     105547   YES 3e6c64f8 (11792 B) -> 105547 B
    6     14     136878     124700   YES 579193f1 (12178 B) -> 124700 B
    7     16     156417     143853   YES c677e286 (12564 B) -> 143853 B

  rep 2 round 7: 156527 named, 143961 unnamable, coverer 255dbdd6 (12566 B)
  rep 3 round 7: 156431 named, 143863 unnamable, coverer d56ed7c7 (12568 B)

24 of 24 rounds: a pack the snapshot already names holds every reachable
object. At round 7 that is 92% of named bytes unnamable with NO
pack-objects run and NOTHING uploaded.

The coverer is the base rebuild's own output, and it grows only with
live content (9,859 -> 12,564 B). forge is ALREADY PAYING for that pack
during normal serving. What the coverage rule withholds is not the pack,
it is the PERMISSION TO UNNAME — and the quiescent window is exactly
what grants it.

## CAVEAT — this rig maximises the hit rate

`base_rebuild_min_secs = 0` here, so a base rebuild is always current.
On shipped defaults it runs far less often, and a covering pack can be
stale relative to recent pushes. This measures that the variant EXISTS
and what it saves when it applies; it does NOT measure the real-world
hit rate, which is unknown.

When no single pack covers, the generalisation is to keep the base plus
every pack named since it and drop the rest. Neither variant is what
TLC checked: the model always builds a fresh coverer.

## Also checked, in the shipped code

- The window is REAL: the UDS listener is bound only after
  `publish(&shared, &sc, Phase::Serving)` (server.rs:267), and when
  `ask` fails the hook writes `ng <ref> the repository server is not
  accepting writes` for EVERY command (hook.rs:216-224). No ref moves.
- A pack CAN still migrate in the window: pre-receive passes, git
  migrates, proc-receive then fails. Unreachable and unnamed, so the
  reclaim collecting it is safe — but the test must state it.
- UNDO DOES NOT CONFLICT. Undo protects pack STEMS in the bucket:
  `undo::referenced` is unioned into the sweep's `named` set
  (fold.rs:965-968), independent of the current snapshot. Unnaming a
  pack the current snapshot holds cannot delete an undo point's pack.
- The reclaim is OPTIONAL and TIME-BOXABLE inside the existing proof:
  `FoldAbandon` exists (ForgeSync.tla:1078) and `ReclaimDone` is enabled
  as soon as no fold is in flight (:471), so a syncer may simply leave.
- THE WINDOW IS ON THE WAKE PATH. `restore()` runs before every
  `Phase::Serving`, not only on a cold start, and a slept forge repo
  wakes on a plain read. The finding doc's "not on the wake path" bullet
  is in tension with putting the reclaim there — unresolved, and the
  zero-work form is what dissolves it.
